Clubfoot
Disabilities & Congenital Disorders
Inward/downward foot turning, Rigid inflexible deformity, Deep medial midfoot crease, Calf muscle atrophy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DCD-013
Reason for Visit: Acute skin breakdown, suspected infection, and severe pain associated with corrective casting.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Infant and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a 5-week-old infant undergoing serial Ponseti casting for bilateral clubfoot, now presenting with relentless, inconsolable crying for 6 hours, localized swelling above the cast, and a foul odor emitting from the plaster. Assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period due to the high risk of ischemic tissue necrosis, compartment syndrome, or deep pressure ulceration requiring immediate cast removal and wound evaluation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: He has been screaming non-stop since lunchtime, his toes look purple, and the cast smells like rotten meat.
History of Present Illness: The parents report a 6-hour history of acute, inconsolable crying in an infant with congenital inward/downward foot turning. The infant is currently in week 3 of serial long-leg plaster casting. Over the past 3 hours, the parents noted the toes on the right foot appearing dusky and swollen. A distinct, foul-smelling odor was noticed emanating from the proximal edge of the right cast an hour ago. The infant has a known rigid inflexible deformity and deep medial midfoot crease. The parents attempted standard soothing techniques and paracetamol without effect, indicating severe underlying pain.
Functional Status: Fully dependent infant; mobility restricted by bilateral long-leg casts.
Social/Safety Concerns: Risk of permanent neurovascular injury if cast compression is not immediately addressed.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 90/55 mmHg | HR: 170 bpm | Temp: 37.8 C | RR: 40 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 4.8 Kg
General: Infant is highly distressed, diaphoretic, and crying vigorously.
Physical Exam: Examination of the right lower extremity reveals a long-leg plaster cast. The toes protruding from the right cast are mildly cyanotic, swollen, and cool to touch, with a capillary refill time of 4 seconds. A foul, purulent odor is obvious at the proximal thigh opening. Following immediate bivalve and removal of the right cast, a 3x3 cm stage III pressure ulcer is visualized over the medial malleolus, with surrounding erythema and purulent exudate. The underlying clubfoot deformity is evident with severe calf muscle atrophy. The left cast and foot appear neurovascularly intact.
Environmental Observations: The house is appropriately heated; infant is wearing loose clothing over the casts.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications. Paracetamol dose confirmed as appropriate for weight.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Clubfoot (complicated by acute cast-induced pressure ulcer and early vascular compromise) - Status: Acute complication
Differentials:

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the immediate improvement in toe perfusion and color upon removal of the mechanical cast compression, and the presence of a localized pressure sore explaining the pain and odor.

Osteomyelitis - Ruled out/unlikely (currently) as the ulcer, while purulent, appears superficial to the bone without systemic signs of sepsis, though risk remains high.

Infant Colic - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of objective focal physical findings (vascular compromise, foul odor, ulcer) directly explaining the inconsolable crying.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The infant presented with signs of acute neurovascular compromise and an infected pressure ulcer secondary to a tight orthopedic cast. The objective findings of cyanotic toes, prolonged capillary refill, and a purulent odor demanded immediate intervention. This assessment could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period because prolonged vascular compression or an untreated necrotic pressure sore would lead to irreversible tissue ischemia, permanent nerve damage, and systemic sepsis, mandating emergency in-home cast removal and wound care.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Performed emergency bivalving and removal of the right long-leg cast using plaster shears. Wound swabbed for culture. Ulcer cleansed with sterile saline and dressed with a non-adherent antimicrobial dressing. Prescribed oral Cephalexin oral suspension.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you inspect the toes every 4 hours for color and warmth, you detect circulation issues early; therefore, setting a timer for these checks ensures the leg remains safe. When you keep the leg elevated on a pillow, gravity reduces the swelling; making this a strict resting position prevents fluid pooling and pain.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral back to the treating pediatric orthopedic clinic for tomorrow morning.
DME/Safety (Optional): Provided extra sterile non-adherent dressings and padding.
Follow-up: Orthopedic clinic review tomorrow morning is mandatory for re-casting and formal wound assessment.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Take the infant directly to the Paediatric Emergency Department or call 000 if the redness around the wound spreads rapidly up the leg, if the infant develops a fever above 38.5 C, or becomes lethargic and stops feeding.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The wound will require ongoing specialized care; the clubfoot correction process is temporarily interrupted.
Clinical Course: Circulation returned to normal immediately upon cast removal. The ulcer will require 1-2 weeks of meticulous dressing changes to heal before casting can safely resume.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek urgent medical care if the infant begins to refuse all feeds or if the toes on the remaining left cast become cold or blue.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The cast slipped or was applied too tightly, cutting off blood flow and rubbing a hole in the skin. Removing it restored blood flow immediately. The antibiotics will treat the local skin infection.
Actionable Steps: Keep the right leg elevated. Administer the antibiotic exactly as charted. Attend the urgent orthopedic appointment tomorrow morning without fail.

